    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        185.04010000000002,
        335.73924479999999,
        325.09431259409916,
        21.446845200000041
      ],
      "text" : "√ is currently undefined as negatives have no square root."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000008,
        321.0990448,
        425.17204538109445,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "This is because if we square a positive or a negative, the answer will be positive."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000036,
        306.33924480000002,
        425.1728689238272,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "Thus we can only take square roots of positive numbers. In another lesson we will"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000036,
        291.69904480000002,
        425.15649256665603,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "define a method we can use to work with and evaluate negative square roots, but"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000036,
        277.05924480000004,
        238.69392059612159,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "for now we will simply say they are undefined."
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.960000000000036,
        250.53934480000004,
        407.01784800000001,
        11.979110400000025
      ],
      "text" : "Not all numbers have a nice even square root. For example, if we found 8"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        476.03970000000004,
        250.53924480000001,
        34.10390837529593,
        22.04695520000007
      ],
      "text" : "√ on"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.95999999999998,
        235.89904480000001,
        425.18762493209596,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "our calculator, the answer would be 2.828427124746190097603377448419... and"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.95999999999998,
        221.25924479999998,
        425.21993163358724,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "even this number is a rounded approximation of the square root. To be as accu-"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.95999999999998,
        206.49904479999998,
        425.25561751674883,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "rate as possible, we will never use the calculator to find decimal approximations of"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.95999999999998,
        191.8592448,
        425.12532741898241,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "square roots. Instead we will express roots in simplest radical form. We will do"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
        84.95999999999998,
        177.21904480000001,
        303.41565525719039,
        11.979110399999996
      ],
      "text" : "this using a property known as the product rule of radicals"
    },
    {
      "fontSize" : 11.955200000000001,
      "monospaced" : false,
      "rect" : [
